  <HOMEWORK title="DDR Homework 8: It only fails sometimes, and only on certain data"
            prompt="This chip's memory test mostly passes - most words check out clean - but a noticeable minority consistently come back wrong, and it isn't random: the same handful of addresses keep failing every run, while most others are completely solid. Write levelling looks fine (CK and DQS are well aligned as a byte lane). What training step is this, if it's not write levelling?"
            hint="Open the chip's editor and look at the per-bit DQ deskew indicators under each DQ column - is it every bit, evenly, or just one or two that stand out from an otherwise well-trained byte lane? A DDR Eye Scope confirms the bus itself is healthy (any untouched pin, like DQ0, locks to a clean, fully open eye) - whatever's wrong with the failing pins isn't visible as a bad signal."
            explanation="Write levelling (the whole-byte-lane DQS-to-CK alignment) is genuinely fine here - the symptom is narrower than that. Two individual DQ pins were left with real per-bit deskew error while the rest of the byte lane is correctly centred - the training step real DDR calls per-bit write-DQ deskew, centring each individual pin's data against DQS rather than the byte lane as a whole. Because only two of eight bits are actually wrong, a captured byte is only corrupted on the specific words where at least one of those two bits happens to differ from the previous word's same bit - which, for ordinary address-counting data, only happens on a minority of addresses. That's exactly why this looks &quot;intermittent&quot; and &quot;only on certain data&quot; rather than totally broken: it's a real, sparser failure signature, and it's the reason real DDR training per-bit deskews every single DQ pin individually rather than assuming one shared, byte-wide alignment (write levelling) is enough. (The per-bit skew lives entirely in how the chip times its own sample of DQ3/DQ6, not in anything an external scope on the bus could flag as bad - which is exactly why probing DQ0 here, rather than one of the actual failing pins, is the faster way to confirm the bus itself is clean. DQ3 and DQ6 toggle too rarely under this BIST pattern for a scope aimed right at them to build a picture quickly anyway.)"/>
</LABCHAIN>
